## Template Organization
- Keep Blade templates clean and focused on presentation
- Structure views in logical directories (layouts, components, partials)
- Use consistent naming conventions for template files
- Break complex templates into smaller, reusable partials
- Separate admin and front-end templates
- Implement template inheritance for consistent layouts

## Components & Reusability
- Use Blade components for reusable UI elements
- Leverage anonymous components for simple UI pieces
- Create component libraries for common elements (buttons, cards, forms)
- Use slots and attributes for component flexibility
- Implement namespaced components for organization

## Security
- Implement proper escaping to prevent XSS vulnerabilities
- Use the {{ }} syntax for escaped output (not {!! !!})
- Use @csrf directive in all forms to prevent CSRF attacks
- Sanitize user input before displaying in templates
- Avoid exposing sensitive data in client-rendered templates
- Implement proper authorization checks in templates

## Performance
- Use template caching in production environments
- Avoid complex logic in Blade templates
- Minimize the use of @php directives
- Use eager loading to prevent N+1 query issues
- Implement view composers for repeated data needs
- Consider fragment caching for expensive template sections

## Structure & Logic
- Use Blade's control structures for conditional rendering
- Leverage @foreach, @forelse, and @empty for collections
- Use view composers for complex data preparation
- Keep JavaScript and CSS in separate files, not in Blade templates
- Utilize stacks for organized script and style inclusion
- Use named routes in templates instead of hardcoded URLs

## Pagination & Data Display
- Use Laravel's built-in pagination views
- Customize pagination templates when needed
- Implement proper empty states for collections
- Use component-based approach for data tables
- Implement proper loading states for async data
- Consider infinite scrolling for large data sets
